 "edits": [
  {
   "op": "replace_brief",
   "brief": "aq-infant-fever",
   "html": "<div class=\"brief aq sp\" id=\"aq-infant-fever\" data-entry=\"hub\" data-lens=\"peds\" data-spine=\"2\" data-shelf=\"fm peds\" data-src=\"aquifer\" data-case=\"Pediatrics 10\" data-bp=\"multi renal\">\n<h4>Fever in an Infant</h4>\n<p class=\"sub\">urine, blood, meninges, lungs, or a virus &middot; red flags &middot; workup by age, appearance and vaccines &middot; the culture confirms<span class=\"subq\">Does the infant look ill, and if well, how old and how vaccinated is the infant?</span></p>\n<nav class=\"sp-step\" aria-label=\"Decision spine\"><a href=\"#aif-s1\"><b>1</b><span>Script</span></a><a href=\"#aif-s2\"><b>2</b><span>Urgent</span></a><a href=\"#aif-s3\"><b>3</b><span>Differential</span></a><a href=\"#aif-s4\"><b>4</b><span>Workup</span></a><a class=\"off\" aria-disabled=\"true\" title=\"Not used in this brief\"><b>5</b><span>Criteria</span></a><a class=\"off\" aria-disabled=\"true\" title=\"Not used in this brief\"><b>6</b><span>Severity</span></a><a class=\"off\" aria-disabled=\"true\" title=\"Not used in this brief\"><b>7</b><span>Treatment</span></a><a class=\"off\" aria-disabled=\"true\" title=\"Not used in this brief\"><b>8</b><span>Follow-up</span></a><a href=\"#aif-bank\" class=\"sp-prac\"><span>Practice</span></a></nav>\n<h5 class=\"tsec sp-h\" id=\"aif-s1\"><b class=\"sp-n\">1</b>Presentation</h5>\n<div class=\"dp\"><span class=\"lbl\">Bottom line</span><ul class=\"bl\"><li>A febrile <b>neonate (28 days or younger)</b> has <b>sepsis until proven otherwise</b>, however well: CBC, blood culture, catheter urine culture and <b>lumbar puncture</b>, then admission on <b>ampicillin plus gentamicin</b>. <button type=\"button\" class=\"sp-pop\" aria-expanded=\"false\" aria-controls=\"aif-pop-1\">In practice</button><span class=\"sp-pop-b\" id=\"aif-pop-1\" hidden>In practice: the 2021 guideline for well-appearing febrile infants 8 to 60 days old, which the Aquifer case teaches, narrows the 28-day rule above: it taps every infant 21 days or younger, and from 22 to 60 days a well infant may skip the lumbar puncture when inflammatory markers (including procalcitonin, C-reactive protein and absolute neutrophil count) are normal.</span></li><li>An <b>ill-appearing</b> infant at any age gets blood and urine cultures and admission, plus a lumbar puncture under 3 months; after the first month the usual drug is <b>IV ceftriaxone</b>.</li><li>A <b>well</b> child of <b>3 to 36 months</b> with no source: <b>fully immunized</b> gets only a <b>catheter urine</b> if at risk for a urinary tract infection (UTI); <b>underimmunized</b> adds a <b>CBC and blood culture</b>.</li></ul></div>\n<p class=\"sp-intro\">When a full history and examination find no focus, a virus is the usual cause; among bacterial sources the urine comes first; the blood, the meninges and the lungs follow. Neonates often do not contain infection locally, and meningeal signs are usually absent under 12 months, so age sets the floor of testing and appearance raises it.</p>\n<h5 class=\"tsec sp-h\" id=\"aif-s2\"><b class=\"sp-n\">2</b>Urgent risks</h5>\n<div class=\"sp-dx\"><table class=\"dense\"><caption>Act before the full history</caption><thead><tr><th>Finding</th><th>Points to</th><th>Action</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Delayed capillary refill, hypotension, altered mental status</td><td><span>Septic shock</span></td><td><span><span class=\"mask\">Support circulation, airway and breathing first; cultures, then IV antibiotics.</span></span></td></tr><tr><td>Fast heart or breathing rate in an ill-appearing infant</td><td><span>Early sepsis</span></td><td><span><span class=\"mask\">Cultures and antibiotics now.</span></span></td></tr><tr><td>Hypothermia or temperature instability in a young infant, poor feeding, lethargy</td><td><span><button type=\"button\" class=\"sp-drw\" data-brief=\"bs-neonatal-sepsis\">Sepsis, with fever absent</button></span></td><td><span><span class=\"mask\">Treat as a febrile infant: full workup and admission.</span></span></td></tr><tr><td>Bulging fontanelle, lethargy, seizure, cries when picked up</td><td><span><button type=\"button\" class=\"sp-drw\" data-brief=\"meningitis\">Bacterial meningitis</button></span></td><td><span><span class=\"mask\">Perform a lumbar puncture.</span></span></td></tr><tr><td>Ill-appearing neonate, or one with skin or mouth vesicles, seizures, or maternal genital herpes</td><td><span>Neonatal herpes simplex virus (HSV) infection</span></td><td><span><span class=\"mask\">Add acyclovir to the antibiotics.</span></span></td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<h5 class=\"tsec sp-h\" id=\"aif-s3\"><b class=\"sp-n\">3</b>Differential</h5>\n<div class=\"sp-dx\"><table class=\"dense\"><caption>Fever in an infant, most tempting first</caption><thead><tr><th>Diagnosis</th><th>Decides it</th><th>Next best step</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><button type=\"button\" class=\"sp-drw\" data-brief=\"peds-uti-recurrent\">Febrile UTI (pyelonephritis)</button></td><td><span>Fever with no focus, fussy and eating less; girl under 2 or uncircumcised boy under 1; fever puts the infection in the kidney.</span></td><td><span><span class=\"mask\">Catheter urinalysis and culture.</span></span></td></tr><tr><td>Occult bacteremia</td><td><span>Positive blood culture in a well-appearing child; the risk is higher if underimmunized.</span></td><td><span><span class=\"mask\">Recall and reexamine when the culture grows.</span></span></td></tr><tr><td><button type=\"button\" class=\"sp-drw\" data-brief=\"meningitis\">Bacterial meningitis</button></td><td><span>Lethargy or irritability, a bulging fontanelle, poor feeding, crying when picked up; neck signs usually absent.</span></td><td><span><span class=\"mask\">Perform a lumbar puncture.</span></span></td></tr><tr><td>Sepsis</td><td><span>Ill-appearing, fast heart and breathing rates, poor perfusion; hypothermia in young infants.</span></td><td><span><span class=\"mask\">Cultures, then IV antibiotics and admission.</span></span></td></tr><tr><td><button type=\"button\" class=\"sp-drw\" data-brief=\"bs-fever-rash-arthralgia\">Roseola (exanthem subitum, sixth disease)</button></td><td><span>3 to 5 days of high fever in a well child under 2, then a rash as the fever breaks; human herpesvirus 6 (HHV-6).</span></td><td><span><span class=\"mask\">Reassure; no tests or treatment.</span></span></td></tr><tr><td>Viral meningitis</td><td><span>Milder; loose stools, rash or cold symptoms; enterovirus.</span></td><td><span><span class=\"mask\">Lumbar puncture: lymphocytes, normal glucose.</span></span></td></tr><tr><td>Pneumonia</td><td><span>Cough, fast breathing, crackles, low saturation; rarely silent; a lower-lobe pneumonia can cause belly pain.</span></td><td><span><span class=\"mask\">Chest x-ray.</span></span></td></tr><tr><td>Acute otitis media</td><td><span>Bulging, poorly mobile, red eardrum; redness alone has no predictive value.</span></td><td><span><span class=\"mask\">A source: treat the ear.</span></span></td></tr><tr><td><button type=\"button\" class=\"sp-drw\" data-brief=\"herpangina\">HSV gingivostomatitis</button></td><td><span>Usually 6 months to 5 years; fever and fussiness, then mouth vesicles and ulcers.</span></td><td><span><span class=\"mask\">Treat the source; Oral Lesions in a Child covers it.</span></span></td></tr><tr><td>Viral upper respiratory infection</td><td><span>Congestion, cough, runny nose.</span></td><td><span><span class=\"mask\">Treat it as a viral source, not fever without one.</span></span></td></tr><tr><td><button type=\"button\" class=\"sp-drw\" data-brief=\"kawasaki\">Kawasaki disease</button></td><td><span>Fever 5 days or more with red eyes, red lips and rash; infants often have incomplete forms.</span></td><td><span><span class=\"mask\">Check the criteria in Kawasaki Disease.</span></span></td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<h5 class=\"tsec sp-h\" id=\"aif-s4\"><b class=\"sp-n\">4</b>Workup</h5>\n<figure class=\"lcp w5a\"><p class=\"kick\">Diagnostic workup</p><p class=\"w5z\">First step</p><div class=\"w5t first\"><div class=\"w5b\"><div class=\"w5c\">No source; girl under 2, uncircumcised boy under 1, circumcised boy under 6 months, prior UTI or urinary tract anomaly, fever over 48 hours, or underimmunized</div><span class=\"w5ar\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&rarr;</span><div class=\"w5o\"><span class=\"mask w5om\"><span class=\"w5ol\">Catheter urinalysis</span></span></div></div><div class=\"w5f\"><div class=\"w5fd f1\"><span class=\"t\" lang=\"en\">Leukocyte esterase and nitrite positive</span><span class=\"ld\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></span></div><span class=\"w5an f1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></span><div class=\"w5m f1\"><span class=\"mask\">UTI strongly suggested: culture and start antibiotics</span></div><div class=\"w5fd\"><span class=\"t\" lang=\"en\">Leukocyte esterase alone</span><span class=\"ld\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></span></div><span class=\"w5an\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></span><div class=\"w5m\"><span class=\"mask\">Pyuria has other causes; the culture decides</span></div><div class=\"w5fd\"><span class=\"t\" lang=\"en\">Nitrite negative</span><span class=\"ld\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></span></div><span class=\"w5an\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></span><div class=\"w5m\"><span class=\"mask\">Does not exclude UTI; infants void often</span></div><div class=\"w5fd fl\"><span class=\"t\" lang=\"en\">Normal</span><span class=\"ld\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></span></div><span class=\"w5an fl\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></span><div class=\"w5m fl\"><span class=\"mask\">UTI unlikely; look elsewhere or follow closely</span></div></div><div class=\"w5b\"><div class=\"w5c\">Well, fully immunized, 3 to 36 months, none of those risks</div><span class=\"w5ar\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&rarr;</span><div class=\"w5o\"><span class=\"mask w5om\"><span class=\"w5ol\">No test: clinical follow-up</span></span></div></div><div class=\"w5f\"><div class=\"w5fd f1 fl\"><span class=\"t\" lang=\"en\">Still febrile after 48 hours</span><span class=\"ld\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></span></div><span class=\"w5an f1 fl\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></span><div class=\"w5m f1 fl\"><span class=\"mask\">Reexamine and test the urine</span></div></div></div><p class=\"w5z\">Targeted tests</p><div class=\"w5t\"><div class=\"w5b\"><div class=\"w5c\">Neonate, 28 days or younger, well or ill</div><span class=\"w5ar\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&rarr;</span><div class=\"w5o\"><span class=\"mask w5om\"><span class=\"w5ol\">CBC, blood culture, catheter urine culture, lumbar puncture</span></span></div></div><div class=\"w5f\"><div class=\"w5fd f1 fl\"><span class=\"t\" lang=\"en\">All sent before antibiotics</span><span class=\"ld\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></span></div><span class=\"w5an f1 fl\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></span><div class=\"w5m f1 fl\"><span class=\"mask\">Admit on empiric antibiotics until a dangerous infection is ruled out</span></div></div><div class=\"w5b\"><div class=\"w5c\">1 to 3 months, ill-appearing or high risk (premature, unvaccinated)</div><span class=\"w5ar\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&rarr;</span><div class=\"w5o\"><span class=\"mask w5om\"><span class=\"w5ol\">CBC, blood and urine cultures, lumbar puncture</span></span></div></div><div class=\"w5f\"><div class=\"w5fd f1 fl\"><span class=\"t\" lang=\"en\">All sent before antibiotics</span><span class=\"ld\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></span></div><span class=\"w5an f1 fl\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></span><div class=\"w5m f1 fl\"><span class=\"mask\">Admit on IV ceftriaxone until serious bacterial infection is ruled out</span></div></div><div class=\"w5b\"><div class=\"w5c\">1 to 3 months, well-appearing <button type=\"button\" class=\"sp-pop\" aria-expanded=\"false\" aria-controls=\"aif-pop-2\">61 to 90 days</button><span class=\"sp-pop-b\" id=\"aif-pop-2\" hidden>Many experts wait for the urinalysis before blood and cerebrospinal fluid (CSF) studies in a well infant of 61 to 90 days.</span></div><span class=\"w5ar\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&rarr;</span><div class=\"w5o\"><span class=\"mask w5om\"><span class=\"w5ol\">Catheter urine, blood culture, inflammatory markers</span></span></div></div><div class=\"w5f\"><div class=\"w5fd f1\"><span class=\"t\" lang=\"en\">All normal</span><span class=\"ld\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></span></div><span class=\"w5an f1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></span><div class=\"w5m f1\"><span class=\"mask\">Home with a recheck in 24 to 36 hours; admit if follow-up is doubtful</span></div><div class=\"w5fd fl\"><span class=\"t\" lang=\"en\">Any abnormal</span><span class=\"ld\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></span></div><span class=\"w5an fl\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></span><div class=\"w5m fl\"><span class=\"mask\">Lumbar puncture, then admission on parenteral antibiotics</span></div></div><div class=\"w5b\"><div class=\"w5c\">3 to 36 months, well, missing a second dose of pneumococcal conjugate vaccine (PCV) or Haemophilus influenzae type b (Hib) vaccine <button type=\"button\" class=\"sp-pop\" aria-expanded=\"false\" aria-controls=\"aif-pop-3\">In practice</button><span class=\"sp-pop-b\" id=\"aif-pop-3\" hidden>In practice: an underimmunized child with fever over 39 C, no source and a white count of 15,000 or more also gets parenteral ceftriaxone pending blood and urine cultures.</span></div><span class=\"w5ar\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&rarr;</span><div class=\"w5o\"><span class=\"mask w5om\"><span class=\"w5ol\">CBC with differential and blood culture, with the urine</span></span></div></div><div class=\"w5f\"><div class=\"w5fd f1\"><span class=\"t\" lang=\"en\">High white count, bands</span><span class=\"ld\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></span></div><span class=\"w5an f1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></span><div class=\"w5m f1\"><span class=\"mask\">More likely bacterial</span></div><div class=\"w5fd fl\"><span class=\"t\" lang=\"en\">Positive days later in a well child</span><span class=\"ld\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></span></div><span class=\"w5an fl\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></span><div class=\"w5m fl\"><span class=\"mask\">Occult bacteremia: recall and reexamine</span></div></div><div class=\"w5b\"><div class=\"w5c\">3 to 36 months, ill-appearing</div><span class=\"w5ar\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&rarr;</span><div class=\"w5o\"><span class=\"mask w5om\"><span class=\"w5ol\">CBC, blood and urine cultures</span></span></div></div><div class=\"w5f\"><div class=\"w5fd f1 fl\"><span class=\"t\" lang=\"en\">All sent before antibiotics</span><span class=\"ld\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></span></div><span class=\"w5an f1 fl\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></span><div class=\"w5m f1 fl\"><span class=\"mask\">Admit on IV ceftriaxone pending cultures</span></div></div><div class=\"w5b\"><div class=\"w5c\">Bulging fontanelle, lethargy, irritability, cries when picked up</div><span class=\"w5ar\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&rarr;</span><div class=\"w5o\"><span class=\"mask w5om\"><span class=\"w5ol\">Lumbar puncture</span></span></div></div><div class=\"w5f\"><div class=\"w5fd f1\"><span class=\"t\" lang=\"en\">Neutrophils, low glucose, high protein</span><span class=\"ld\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></span></div><span class=\"w5an f1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></span><div class=\"w5m f1\"><span class=\"mask\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"sp-drw\" data-brief=\"meningitis\">Bacterial meningitis</button></span></div><div class=\"w5fd fl\"><span class=\"t\" lang=\"en\">Lymphocytes, normal glucose</span><span class=\"ld\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></span></div><span class=\"w5an fl\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></span><div class=\"w5m fl\"><span class=\"mask\">Viral meningitis</span></div></div><div class=\"w5b\"><div class=\"w5c\">Cough, fast breathing, crackles; or fever over 39 C with a white count of 20,000 or more and no other source</div><span class=\"w5ar\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&rarr;</span><div class=\"w5o\"><span class=\"mask w5om\"><span class=\"w5ol\">Chest x-ray</span></span></div></div><div class=\"w5f\"><div class=\"w5fd f1 fl\"><span class=\"t\" lang=\"en\">Infiltrate</span><span class=\"ld\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></span></div><span class=\"w5an f1 fl\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></span><div class=\"w5m f1 fl\"><span class=\"mask\">Pneumonia</span></div></div></div><p class=\"w5z\">Diagnostic test</p><div class=\"w5t\"><div class=\"w5b\"><div class=\"w5c\">Positive urinalysis</div><span class=\"w5ar\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&rarr;</span><div class=\"w5o\"><span class=\"mask w5om\"><span class=\"w5ol\">Catheter urine culture</span></span></div></div><div class=\"w5f\"><div class=\"w5fd f1 fl\"><span class=\"t\" lang=\"en\">A single uropathogen</span><span class=\"ld\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></span></div><span class=\"w5an f1 fl\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></span><div class=\"w5m f1 fl\"><span class=\"mask\">UTI; E. coli in 85 to 90%, the rest Klebsiella, Proteus or enterococcus</span></div></div><div class=\"w5b\"><div class=\"w5c\">Blood drawn for an ill or underimmunized infant</div><span class=\"w5ar\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&rarr;</span><div class=\"w5o\"><span class=\"mask w5om\"><span class=\"w5ol\">Blood culture</span></span></div></div><div class=\"w5f\"><div class=\"w5fd f1\"><span class=\"t\" lang=\"en\">Positive in a well child</span><span class=\"ld\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></span></div><span class=\"w5an f1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></span><div class=\"w5m f1\"><span class=\"mask\">Occult bacteremia</span></div><div class=\"w5fd fl\"><span class=\"t\" lang=\"en\">Positive in an ill child</span><span class=\"ld\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></span></div><span class=\"w5an fl\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></span><div class=\"w5m fl\"><span class=\"mask\">Sepsis</span></div></div><div class=\"w5b\"><div class=\"w5c\">Lumbar puncture done</div><span class=\"w5ar\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&rarr;</span><div class=\"w5o\"><span class=\"mask w5om\"><span class=\"w5ol\">CSF culture</span></span></div></div><div class=\"w5f\"><div class=\"w5fd f1 fl\"><span class=\"t\" lang=\"en\">Positive, even when the Gram stain is negative</span><span class=\"ld\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></span></div><span class=\"w5an f1 fl\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></span><div class=\"w5m f1 fl\"><span class=\"mask\">Bacterial meningitis</span></div></div><div class=\"w5b\"><div class=\"w5c\">3 to 5 days of high fever in a well child</div><span class=\"w5ar\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&rarr;</span><div class=\"w5o\"><span class=\"mask w5om\"><span class=\"w5ol\">None: clinical</span></span></div></div><div class=\"w5f\"><div class=\"w5fd f1 fl\"><span class=\"t\" lang=\"en\">Rash as the fever breaks</span><span class=\"ld\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></span></div><span class=\"w5an f1 fl\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></span><div class=\"w5m f1 fl\"><span class=\"mask\">Roseola</span></div></div></div><p class=\"w5skip\"><span class=\"w5z\">Skip</span><span class=\"mask\">A CBC in a well, fully immunized child (a high count is almost always a false positive); a bag urine for culture (contaminated, screening only); group A strep testing under age 3; a lumbar puncture in a well child over 3 months with no meningeal signs.</span></p></figure>\n<div class=\"sp-dx\"><table class=\"dense\"><caption>Treatment by cause</caption><thead><tr><th>Cause</th><th>Setting</th><th>Treatment</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Febrile neonate, any source, before cultures return</td><td><span><span class=\"mask\">Admit</span></span></td><td><span><span class=\"mask\">IV ampicillin plus gentamicin; ampicillin plus cefotaxime if meningitis is suspected; add acyclovir for herpes signs or an ill appearance. No ceftriaxone under 1 month: it displaces bilirubin. These target the common neonatal pathogens: group B streptococcus, E. coli and Listeria. <button type=\"button\" class=\"sp-drw\" data-brief=\"meningitis\">Bacterial Meningitis</button> covers the meningitis regimen.</span></span></td></tr><tr><td>Ill-appearing infant over 1 month</td><td><span><span class=\"mask\">Admit</span></span></td><td><span><span class=\"mask\">IV ceftriaxone until serious bacterial infection is ruled out; vancomycin joins it when meningitis is suspected (Bacterial Meningitis).</span></span></td></tr><tr><td>Febrile UTI in an older infant or child, well, keeping oral medicine down, reliable family</td><td><span><span class=\"mask\">Home</span></span></td><td><span><span class=\"mask\">An oral cephalosporin (cefixime or cephalexin); not nitrofurantoin, which reaches an acceptable level only in the urine. <button type=\"button\" class=\"sp-pop\" aria-expanded=\"false\" aria-controls=\"aif-pop-4\">Dosing</button><span class=\"sp-pop-b\" id=\"aif-pop-4\" hidden>Weight times milligrams per kilogram per day, split by the number of daily doses; choose the fewest doses a day and a volume in whole milliliters.</span></span></span></td></tr><tr><td>Febrile UTI, toxic, vomiting doses, or doubtful adherence</td><td><span><span class=\"mask\">Admit</span></span></td><td><span><span class=\"mask\">IV ceftriaxone; switch to ampicillin if enterococcus grows, since ceftriaxone misses it.</span></span></td></tr><tr><td>After a first febrile UTI under age 2</td><td><span><span class=\"mask\">Follow-up</span></span></td><td><span><span class=\"mask\">Renal and bladder ultrasound; <button type=\"button\" class=\"sp-drw\" data-brief=\"peds-uti-recurrent\">Urinary Tract Infection in a Child</button> covers the voiding cystourethrogram (VCUG).</span></span></td></tr><tr><td>Occult bacteremia, now well</td><td><span><span class=\"mask\">Recall</span></span></td><td><span><span class=\"mask\">Reexamine when the blood culture grows.</span></span></td></tr><tr><td>Roseola</td><td><span><span class=\"mask\">Home</span></span></td><td><span><span class=\"mask\">Reassurance; no treatment.</span></span></td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"sp-dx\"><table class=\"dense\"><caption>Clues that change the answer</caption><thead><tr><th>Clue</th><th>Points to</th><th>Next best step</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Fussy and eating less, nothing else</td><td><span><span class=\"mask\">Separates nothing: UTI, meningitis and viruses all do it</span></span></td><td><span><span class=\"mask\">Use age, appearance and vaccines to set the tests.</span></span></td></tr><tr><td>Missing a second dose of PCV or Hib</td><td><span><span class=\"mask\">Underimmunized: bacteremia risk higher</span></span></td><td><span><span class=\"mask\">Add CBC and blood culture.</span></span></td></tr><tr><td>Diphtheria, tetanus and pertussis or pneumococcal vaccine in the last few days</td><td><span><span class=\"mask\">Immunization fever</span></span></td><td><span><span class=\"mask\">Watch: it comes within a few days; a live vaccine (measles, mumps and rubella, or varicella) brings fever at 6-14 days.</span></span></td></tr><tr><td>Circumcised boy 6 months or older, immunized, well</td><td><span><span class=\"mask\">Low UTI risk</span></span></td><td><span><span class=\"mask\">Clinical follow-up, no urine.</span></span></td></tr><tr><td>No Kernig or Brudzinski sign under 12 months</td><td><span><span class=\"mask\">Excludes nothing: usually absent in infant meningitis</span></span></td><td><span><span class=\"mask\">Examine the fontanelle, feeding and alertness.</span></span></td></tr><tr><td>Eardrum red but mobile and not bulging</td><td><span><span class=\"mask\">Not acute otitis media</span></span></td><td><span><span class=\"mask\">Keep working it up as fever without a source.</span></span></td></tr><tr><td>High white count in a well, immunized child</td><td><span><span class=\"mask\">Almost always a false positive</span></span></td><td><span><span class=\"mask\">Avoid further blood tests.</span></span></td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"sp-pause\"><b class=\"pp-t\">Pause point</b>\n<div class=\"pp-b\">\n<span class=\"sl\">6-month-old girl, well, fully immunized, febrile, no source</span>\n<span class=\"si pp-off\">Offered as <em>CBC and blood culture</em> or <em>lumbar puncture</em></span>\n<span class=\"si\"><span class=\"mask\">Ill-appearing or underimmunized? At risk for UTI?</span></span>\n<span class=\"pp-a\">&rarr; <span class=\"mask\">Catheter urinalysis and culture only: a girl under 2 is at risk</span></span>\n</div>\n<div class=\"pp-b\">\n<span class=\"sl\">Febrile neonate, feeding well and alert</span>\n<span class=\"si pp-off\">Offered as <em>home with a recheck</em> or <em>urine only</em></span>\n<span class=\"si\"><span class=\"mask\">28 days or younger?</span></span>\n<span class=\"pp-a\">&rarr; <span class=\"mask\">Full workup with lumbar puncture, admission, ampicillin plus gentamicin</span></span>\n</div>\n<div class=\"pp-b\">\n<span class=\"sl\">Febrile UTI, still febrile on ceftriaxone; enterococcus grows</span>\n<span class=\"si pp-off\">Offered as <em>add gentamicin</em> or <em>continue ceftriaxone</em></span>\n<span class=\"si\"><span class=\"mask\">Does the drug cover the organism?</span></span>\n<span class=\"pp-a\">&rarr; <span class=\"mask\">Switch to ampicillin</span></span>\n</div>\n</div>\n\n<h5 class=\"authored-hdr\" id=\"aif-bank\">Practice</h5>\n<ol class=\"bank authored\">\n<li data-type=\"dx\" data-src=\"aquifer\" data-d1=\"Cystitis\" data-d2=\"Occult bacteremia\" data-item-id=\"q_cf81fbb55a02303d3e5e\" data-item-version=\"1\" data-item-status=\"ready\" data-key-id=\"o_35df5f7ac76b7fc9cbbb\" data-d1-id=\"o_ed6dee6070b88b54f25f\" data-d2-id=\"o_cac9856a3490ba917b3f\" data-key-why=\"Fever with positive esterase and nitrite in an infant is a febrile UTI, which is pyelonephritis.\" data-d1-why=\"Cystitis is a bladder infection; fever puts the infection in the kidney.\" data-d2-why=\"Occult bacteremia is a positive blood culture in a well child; a positive urine names the source.\">6-month-old girl with 2 days of fever to 39.4 C, fussier than usual and taking less formula; no cough, runny nose, vomiting or diarrhea; vaccines up to date; well-appearing with a normal examination, including a flat fontanelle, clear lungs and normal eardrums; catheterized urinalysis shows positive leukocyte esterase and nitrite with many white cells &rarr; <b>Pyelonephritis</b> &rarr; a febrile UTI in an infant is an upper-tract infection; catheter culture next</li>\n<li data-type=\"test\" data-src=\"authored\" data-d1=\"Lumbar puncture\" data-d2=\"CBC and blood culture\" data-item-id=\"q_0887cb32b9f3ac96a642\" data-item-version=\"1\" data-item-status=\"ready\" data-key-id=\"o_e41b6efaac3cf6825b52\" data-d1-id=\"o_2806c6ada2ecf7fb3ef6\" data-d2-id=\"o_926c30c508967d3ea4a4\" data-lead-in=\"Which of the following is the most appropriate diagnostic study to obtain at this time?\" data-key-why=\"A girl under 2 with fever and no source is at risk for UTI, the commonest bacterial source: catheter urinalysis.\" data-d1-why=\"A well child over 3 months with no meningeal signs needs no lumbar puncture.\" data-d2-why=\"A well, fully immunized child needs no CBC or blood culture.\">7-month-old girl with 2 days of fever to 39.2 C and no other symptoms; vaccines up to date; playful and well hydrated, with a normal fontanelle, clear lungs, normal eardrums and no rash &rarr; <b>Urinalysis by catheter</b> &rarr; UTI is the commonest serious bacterial infection behind a fever without a source</li>\n<li data-type=\"next\" data-src=\"authored\" data-d1=\"Catheter urinalysis now\" data-d2=\"CBC and blood culture\" data-item-id=\"q_4ec1d4d4aea300eeb5be\" data-item-version=\"1\" data-item-status=\"ready\" data-key-id=\"o_8861b07f05eed711a6bd\" data-d1-id=\"o_b03ac70f03159b29322b\" data-d2-id=\"o_7a412e175e23c8d5a498\" data-key-why=\"A circumcised boy 6 months or older, immunized and well, with no other risk: clinical follow-up without testing.\" data-d1-why=\"Urine testing is for girls under 2, uncircumcised boys under 1, circumcised boys under 6 months, prior UTI or urinary tract anomaly, or fever over 48 hours.\" data-d2-why=\"A well, fully immunized child needs no CBC or blood culture.\">10-month-old circumcised boy with 1 day of fever to 38.9 C; vaccines up to date; no prior urinary infection or known urinary tract anomaly; playful and drinking well, with a normal examination &rarr; <b>Clinical follow-up alone</b> &rarr; low UTI risk and well-appearing: no testing needed</li>\n<li data-type=\"test\" data-src=\"authored\" data-d1=\"Catheter urine studies alone\" data-d2=\"Lumbar puncture\" data-item-id=\"q_a4392692c4e94c7797db\" data-item-version=\"1\" data-item-status=\"ready\" data-key-id=\"o_b61b9bd09d4aaf7ea3cc\" data-d1-id=\"o_99cb601c2c955b4507c8\" data-d2-id=\"o_ab1251131e1d314e2811\" data-lead-in=\"Which of the following is the most appropriate diagnostic study to obtain at this time?\" data-key-why=\"Missing a second dose of PCV or Hib: bacteremia is likelier, so CBC and blood culture join the urine.\" data-d1-why=\"Urine alone is the plan only for a well, fully immunized child.\" data-d2-why=\"A well child over 3 months with no meningeal signs needs no lumbar puncture.\">9-month-old boy whose parents declined all vaccines, with 1 day of fever to 39.6 C and no other symptoms; alert and playful, with a flat fontanelle, supple neck and normal examination &rarr; <b>CBC, blood culture, and urine</b> &rarr; underimmunized: bacteremia is more likely, so blood tests join the urine</li>\n<li data-type=\"dx\" data-src=\"authored\" data-d1=\"Sepsis\" data-d2=\"Bacterial meningitis\" data-item-id=\"q_c0e480002ab8e157aac0\" data-item-version=\"1\" data-item-status=\"ready\" data-key-id=\"o_11caa0dfe017d255b22a\" data-d1-id=\"o_b66128023292d78ddc14\" data-d2-id=\"o_826ec393a5cddbb7d4e9\" data-key-why=\"A positive blood culture in a well-appearing child is occult bacteremia: recall and reexamine.\" data-d1-why=\"Sepsis is an ill-appearing child with fast heart and breathing rates and poor perfusion.\" data-d2-why=\"Bacterial meningitis brings irritability or lethargy and a bulging fontanelle.\">12-month-old unvaccinated boy with fever to 39.8 C and no source on examination; a blood culture drawn in clinic grows gram-positive diplococci the next day, when he is afebrile, playful and eating well, with a normal fontanelle and no rash &rarr; <b>Occult bacteremia</b> &rarr; a positive blood culture in a well-appearing child; recall and reexamine</li>\n<li data-type=\"claim\" data-src=\"authored\" data-d1=\"Makes meningitis unlikely\" data-d2=\"Excludes bacterial meningitis\" data-item-id=\"q_043a3ad818d97ed85b05\" data-item-version=\"1\" data-item-status=\"ready\" data-key-id=\"o_c3ca1b530ddceb3b01ca\" data-d1-id=\"o_781ef21fd8d7f81ca135\" data-d2-id=\"o_43fceb1fd9a850c4b92b\" data-lead-in=\"Which of the following is the most appropriate interpretation of this finding?\" data-key-why=\"Most infants under 12 months with bacterial meningitis have no Kernig or Brudzinski sign, so their absence excludes nothing.\" data-d1-why=\"The signs are absent in most infants with meningitis, so their absence cannot make it unlikely.\" data-d2-why=\"A negative Brudzinski sign does not rule out bacterial meningitis; the lumbar puncture decides.\">5-month-old boy with 1 day of fever, irritability and poor feeding; neck supple; Kernig and Brudzinski signs absent &rarr; <b>Does not exclude meningitis</b> &rarr; these signs are usually absent in infants with meningitis</li>\n<li data-type=\"test\" data-src=\"authored\" data-d1=\"Blood culture\" data-d2=\"CSF culture\" data-item-id=\"q_8b50a73cd19a8b9bcf92\" data-item-version=\"1\" data-item-status=\"ready\" data-key-id=\"o_f78340efa8e87333f5d4\" data-d1-id=\"o_470a8c7cc6f9f9734dfe\" data-d2-id=\"o_792fe2903d5a73956545\" data-lead-in=\"Which of the following laboratory studies is most likely to confirm the diagnosis?\" data-key-why=\"Esterase and nitrite strongly suggest UTI; the catheter urine culture confirms it and names the organism.\" data-d1-why=\"A blood culture confirms bacteremia, not a urine source.\" data-d2-why=\"A CSF culture confirms meningitis; a well child with a normal fontanelle needs no tap.\">7-month-old girl with fever to 39.3 C, no source on examination and a normal fontanelle; vaccines up to date; catheterized urinalysis shows pyuria with positive leukocyte esterase and nitrite &rarr; <b>Urine culture</b> &rarr; the catheter culture confirms the UTI and names the organism</li>\n<li data-type=\"dx\" data-src=\"authored\" data-d1=\"Viral meningitis\" data-d2=\"Tuberculous meningitis\" data-item-id=\"q_ba095e97628c1fd44705\" data-item-version=\"1\" data-item-status=\"ready\" data-key-id=\"o_e7ac3620eae5eb5312c3\" data-d1-id=\"o_97198d5da7bdab00aea4\" data-d2-id=\"o_83d4c6a1be1fa6c8c527\" data-key-why=\"Neutrophils, low glucose and high protein are bacterial meningitis; a negative Gram stain does not exclude it.\" data-d1-why=\"Viral meningitis shows lymphocytes with a normal glucose and a milder illness.\" data-d2-why=\"Neutrophils with a low glucose in an acutely ill infant are the bacterial pattern; Bacterial Meningitis covers the other CSF patterns.\">8-month-old girl with 2 days of fever, lethargy and vomiting and a bulging fontanelle; cerebrospinal fluid shows a high white cell count, mostly neutrophils, low glucose and high protein; Gram stain shows no organisms &rarr; <b>Bacterial meningitis</b> &rarr; a negative Gram stain does not exclude it; the culture confirms</li>\n<li data-type=\"next\" data-src=\"authored\" data-d1=\"Oral amoxicillin\" data-d2=\"Blood culture\" data-item-id=\"q_c30b37eca2ac168a7475\" data-item-version=\"1\" data-item-status=\"ready\" data-key-id=\"o_125f3a5dd3da82f6a44b\" data-d1-id=\"o_641825788a24c6c2980c\" data-d2-id=\"o_5f38fbbeb0d5a4c1b6e1\" data-key-why=\"Three to five days of high fever in a well child, then a rash as the fever breaks, is roseola: reassure.\" data-d1-why=\"Roseola is viral and self-limited; antibiotics add nothing.\" data-d2-why=\"Roseola is a clinical diagnosis; a well child needs no blood culture.\">10-month-old boy with 4 days of fever to 40 C who stayed playful and fed well throughout; the fever stopped yesterday and today he has a pink, blanching rash on the trunk; examination otherwise normal &rarr; <b>Reassurance</b> &rarr; roseola (HHV-6): no tests or treatment needed</li>\n<li data-type=\"claim\" data-src=\"authored\" data-d1=\"Likely occult bacteremia\" data-d2=\"Needs a lumbar puncture\" data-item-id=\"q_e412ee0850cc0bde9b60\" data-item-version=\"1\" data-item-status=\"ready\" data-key-id=\"o_58604bc06b1e439fc5f2\" data-d1-id=\"o_9d713a09f1ca12db7963\" data-d2-id=\"o_43a4daaee0c3b1fd931a\" data-lead-in=\"Which of the following is the most appropriate interpretation of this result?\" data-key-why=\"Bacteremia is rare in a well, fully immunized child, so a high count is almost always a false positive.\" data-d1-why=\"Occult bacteremia is a positive blood culture, not a high white count.\" data-d2-why=\"A well child over 3 months with no meningeal signs needs no lumbar puncture.\">9-month-old fully immunized girl with fever to 39 C for 1 day; well-appearing and playful, with a normal examination; a CBC obtained at urgent care shows a white count of 17,000 with a mild left shift &rarr; <b>Probably a false positive</b> &rarr; bacteremia is rare in a well, immunized child, so the count predicts little</li>\n<li data-type=\"avoid\" data-src=\"authored\" data-d1=\"Culture of a catheter specimen\" data-d2=\"Suprapubic aspiration for culture\" data-item-id=\"q_b223cc7099c698b5c451\" data-item-version=\"1\" data-item-status=\"ready\" data-key-id=\"o_29a31d8c87c5101b0b73\" data-d1-id=\"o_39ad7db486e87adaab45\" data-d2-id=\"o_11001ba508d93611b0a2\" data-key-why=\"A bag specimen is contaminated too often to culture; it is for screening only.\" data-d1-why=\"A catheter specimen is the way to culture urine in a child who is not toilet trained.\" data-d2-why=\"Suprapubic aspiration is a valid culture specimen when catheterization is not possible.\">6-month-old girl with fever and no source on examination, not toilet trained; a urine specimen is needed to rule out infection &rarr; <b>Culture of a bag specimen</b> &rarr; high contamination rate; a bag specimen is for screening only</li>\n<li data-type=\"next\" data-src=\"authored\" data-d1=\"Admit for IV ceftriaxone\" data-d2=\"Oral nitrofurantoin at home\" data-item-id=\"q_82414b670b2f966bd2c9\" data-item-version=\"1\" data-item-status=\"ready\" data-key-id=\"o_b10fdfe9146bc22d8e5b\" data-d1-id=\"o_348b53e8cbe837fedf90\" data-d2-id=\"o_c5f8863412cc4c79a249\" data-key-why=\"A well infant who keeps oral medicine down, with a reliable family, takes an oral cephalosporin at home.\" data-d1-why=\"IV therapy is for a toxic infant, one who cannot keep oral medicine down, or doubtful adherence.\" data-d2-why=\"Nitrofurantoin reaches only the urine, so it cannot treat pyelonephritis.\">6-month-old girl with pyelonephritis on catheterized urinalysis, culture pending; well-appearing, taking formula and keeping it down; reliable parents with a phone and transportation &rarr; <b>Oral cephalexin at home</b> &rarr; most febrile UTIs are treated orally</li>\n<li data-type=\"next\" data-src=\"authored\" data-d1=\"Switch to oral trimethoprim-sulfamethoxazole\" data-d2=\"Continue oral cephalexin\" data-item-id=\"q_b81574f85ccef5a0b55a\" data-item-version=\"2\" data-item-status=\"ready\" data-key-id=\"o_544c9cdb8d507bfe4d43\" data-d1-id=\"o_e06e6439869432cb26d9\" data-d2-id=\"o_f4b3cd7d40838e47d7f2\" data-key-why=\"Vomiting every dose with poor perfusion: admit for IV ceftriaxone.\" data-d1-why=\"An infant who cannot keep oral medicine down needs IV therapy, not another oral drug.\" data-d2-why=\"Continuing oral therapy ignores the vomiting and the poor perfusion.\">6-month-old girl started on oral cephalexin for pyelonephritis 1 day ago now vomits every dose, is sleepier and has cool hands with a capillary refill of 3 seconds &rarr; <b>Admit for IV ceftriaxone</b> &rarr; toxic, or unable to keep oral medicine down</li>\n<li data-type=\"avoid\" data-src=\"authored\" data-d1=\"Cephalexin\" data-d2=\"Trimethoprim-sulfamethoxazole\" data-item-id=\"q_098f4b7134f69f86a17b\" data-item-version=\"1\" data-item-status=\"ready\" data-key-id=\"o_52c03680fce0d912dfaa\" data-d1-id=\"o_b7c9db4ce3fd38bd676b\" data-d2-id=\"o_cf2e46991561fa88db30\" data-key-why=\"Nitrofurantoin reaches therapeutic levels only in the urine, not the kidney.\" data-d1-why=\"Cephalexin, an oral cephalosporin, treats a febrile UTI.\" data-d2-why=\"Trimethoprim-sulfamethoxazole treats a susceptible febrile UTI, though not as a first choice.\">7-month-old boy with fever to 39.5 C and a catheter urine culture growing E. coli susceptible to all oral agents tested; well-appearing and ready to go home on oral therapy &rarr; <b>Nitrofurantoin</b> &rarr; therapeutic levels in the urine only, not the kidney</li>\n<li data-type=\"next\" data-src=\"authored\" data-d1=\"Add gentamicin\" data-d2=\"Continue ceftriaxone\" data-item-id=\"q_fdc2e0aecda7d121b109\" data-item-version=\"1\" data-item-status=\"ready\" data-key-id=\"o_452e63fc40fe0b814093\" data-d1-id=\"o_3e99f057d3e25af20818\" data-d2-id=\"o_486a7876216c902a6cfe\" data-key-why=\"Ceftriaxone does not cover enterococcus; ampicillin does.\" data-d1-why=\"Gentamicin is never used alone and does not fix the missing enterococcal cover.\" data-d2-why=\"Ceftriaxone misses enterococcus, so the fever persists.\">5-month-old girl admitted on IV ceftriaxone for pyelonephritis is still febrile after 48 hours; the catheter urine culture grows Enterococcus faecalis susceptible to ampicillin &rarr; <b>Switch to ampicillin</b> &rarr; ceftriaxone does not cover enterococcus</li>\n<li data-type=\"next\" data-src=\"authored\" data-d1=\"8 mL twice daily\" data-d2=\"2 mL twice daily\" data-item-id=\"q_3ee904c7ec2e5e6ffb78\" data-item-version=\"1\" data-item-status=\"ready\" data-key-id=\"o_20aa7b9d6ef9ff666cd1\" data-d1-id=\"o_98dc00e33fb48b61873d\" data-d2-id=\"o_d5532761afbfdfef0bcc\" data-lead-in=\"Which of the following is the correct volume for each dose?\" data-key-why=\"50 mg/kg/day for 8 kg is 400 mg a day, 200 mg a dose; at 50 mg per mL that is 4 mL.\" data-d1-why=\"8 mL is 400 mg a dose, the whole daily dose given twice.\" data-d2-why=\"2 mL is 100 mg a dose, half the dose.\">8-kg infant is prescribed an oral antibiotic at 50 mg/kg/day divided every 12 hours; the suspension contains 250 mg per 5 mL &rarr; <b>4 mL twice daily</b> &rarr; 400 mg a day, 200 mg a dose, at 50 mg per mL</li>\n<li data-type=\"next\" data-src=\"authored\" data-d1=\"Voiding cystourethrogram\" data-d2=\"No imaging at this age\" data-item-id=\"q_4933bcf168fd3adde999\" data-item-version=\"1\" data-item-status=\"ready\" data-key-id=\"o_1de359f6d7596080e693\" data-d1-id=\"o_d91c9add3e9b8ed02960\" data-d2-id=\"o_30b374f2b3e5ed75e86c\" data-key-why=\"A first febrile UTI under age 2 gets a renal and bladder ultrasound.\" data-d1-why=\"The VCUG waits for an abnormal ultrasound, fever of 39 C or more with an organism other than E. coli, or a second febrile UTI.\" data-d2-why=\"Under age 2 a first febrile UTI does need the ultrasound.\">6-month-old girl with her first febrile UTI, E. coli on catheter culture, afebrile within 48 hours on oral therapy and now finishing the course; normal growth and blood pressure &rarr; <b>Renal and bladder ultrasound</b> &rarr; first febrile UTI under age 2; VCUG only if abnormal or recurrent</li>\n<li data-type=\"test\" data-src=\"authored\" data-d1=\"Lumbar puncture\" data-d2=\"Abdominal ultrasound\" data-item-id=\"q_1608e3829bcc736d5831\" data-item-version=\"1\" data-item-status=\"ready\" data-key-id=\"o_43612d0a0ff9eeadfe87\" data-d1-id=\"o_126086eace80e8d3c5cd\" data-d2-id=\"o_589fc7713ffef5c4b92d\" data-lead-in=\"Which of the following is the most appropriate diagnostic study to obtain at this time?\" data-key-why=\"Fever over 39 C with a white count of 20,000 or more and no other source calls for a chest x-ray.\" data-d1-why=\"A well child over 3 months with no meningeal signs needs no lumbar puncture.\" data-d2-why=\"A lower-lobe pneumonia can cause belly pain; the chest film finds it.\">9-month-old boy with 2 days of fever to 39.6 C, no cough, fussy with feeds and a mildly uncomfortable belly; vaccines up to date; well-appearing, breathing comfortably, lungs clear; catheterized urinalysis normal; white count 22,000 &rarr; <b>Chest x-ray</b> &rarr; occult pneumonia: high fever and a white count of 20,000 or more with no other source</li>\n</ol>\n<div class=\"pearls\"><span class=\"lbl\">Pairs with</span> <b>Fever and Sepsis in the Newborn</b> covers the septic newborn. <b>Bacterial Meningitis</b> covers the CSF grid and the meningitis regimen by age. <b>Urinary Tract Infection in a Child</b> covers the VCUG, reflux and recurrence after a febrile UTI. <b>Fever and Rash in a Child</b> covers roseola among the rashes. <b>Oral Lesions in a Child</b> covers HSV gingivostomatitis. <b>Kawasaki Disease</b> covers fever of 5 days or more with red eyes and lips. Ask: does the infant look ill, and if well, how old and how vaccinated?</div>\n<div class=\"rule\"><span class=\"lbl\">Transferable rule</span> In an infant with fever and no source, appearance, age and vaccine status decide how much testing is needed. Test the urine of any child at risk, and collect it by catheter. The culture, not the dipstick, confirms the infection.</div>\n<div class=\"traps\"><div class=\"trapline\"><span class=\"pill p-salient\">Salient decoy</span><span class=\"trapwhy\">Fussiness and poor feeding come with UTI, meningitis and viral illness alike and cannot separate them.</span></div><div class=\"trapline\"><span class=\"pill p-attr\">Unchecked attribute</span><span class=\"trapwhy\">A supple neck and no Kernig or Brudzinski sign under 12 months do not exclude meningitis.</span></div><div class=\"trapline\"><span class=\"pill p-test\">Test-overrides-findings</span><span class=\"trapwhy\">A high white count in a well, fully immunized infant is almost always a false positive.</span></div><div class=\"trapline\"><span class=\"pill p-class\">Class-vs-member</span><span class=\"trapwhy\">Nitrofurantoin treats bladder infection but never reaches the kidney, so it cannot treat a febrile UTI.</span></div></div>\n</div>",
   "ledger": "repair/migration/id/aq-infant-fever/spine/claim_map.json",
   "note": "decision spine v2: aq-infant-fever"
  }
 ]
}